ause there  may be many  at the same time there's also been in  the last 25 years a lot of  investigations scientifically of  spiritual practices some of these are of  brain scans and physiology some of them  are social science studies of the  effects of spiritual practices in 2001  an enormous volume called the handbook  of religion and health came out  reviewing about 1500 papers in  peer-reviewed journals about religious  and spiritual practices and in 2012 vol  2 reviewed a further and nearly 2,000  papers and these are huge volumes and a  huge amount of research about 4 percent  of these papers found that religious  practices could make people feel worse  and these were for people who lived in a  state of religious conflict or who felt  incredibly guilty and belonged to  religions that made them feel even  guiltier but the great majority of these  studies showed beneficial effects  basically they showed that people who  have religious and spiritual practices  are happier healthier and live longer  the effects on lifespan were quite  dramatic and as some people have said if  any pill were discovered that had these  effects it would be hailed as a wonder  drug these findings have not been lost  on atheists the atheist writer and  philosopher Alain de Botton  brought out a book three or four years  ago called religion for atheists and  what de Botton says in that book is that  he himself was raised atheist his  parents were Jewish living in  Switzerland and were fanatically  atheistic he said that if anyone showed  the slightest interest in religion his  parents treated them as if they were  suffering from some terminal  degenerative disease and never took them  seriously thereafter and so he said he  was raised atheist he accepts his  parental faith but he's not interested  in fighting religion but in atheism to  point not what happens when you've given  that religion you no longer gather in a  sacred place you no longer have seen  together with members of your c